WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:06.823
I'm Melissa.

00:00:06.823 --> 00:00:08.240
And today, I'm
going to be talking

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.540
about how to achieve a seamless
workflow between R, Python,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.690
and SAS, all
from within RStudio.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.860
Now we're at an
RStudio conference.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.830
And more specifically,
right now,

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:21.230
we're in a session that's
called Some of my Best Friends

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.550
Use Python.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.040
So I already know that
everyone in this room

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.650
loves at least three
of the words that

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.470
are in the title of my talk.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.900
But before we talk
about R or Python,

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.460
I first want to take
a moment to talk

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.002
about something that
I know we're all

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.620
even more passionate about.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.960
Which, of course, is SAS.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:42.960
Don't worry.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.220
I'm totally kidding.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.650
I was not referring
to SAS here at all.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.220
I was actually
talking about food.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.840
And I apologize in
advance because I

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.923
know we're still about an
hour away from our lunch

00:00:52.923 --> 00:00:53.580
break today.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.040
But I do just want
to take a moment

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.830
to talk about food
before we go any further.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:02.070
I love food, but I'm
not a very good cook.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.625
So I really like
these meal kit boxes

00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:05.250
that you can get
delivered to your door

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.532
because the recipes are
always super straightforward

00:01:07.532 --> 00:01:09.240
and they always come
with the exact right

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.200
ingredients measured out in
the exact right proportions.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.330
And usually if I follow
along with the steps

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:17.790
in the recipe in one of
these meal kit boxes,

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.240
I can get the results to look
pretty close to the pictures

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.860
that are in the recipe.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.610
But there's just one problem
with these meal kit boxes,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.870
and that problem is that
they give bad cooks like me.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.750
False confidence.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.550
These meal kit boxes
have me thinking

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.400
that I'm totally capable of
recreating these masterpieces

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.760
totally on my own.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.350
But a few weeks later, if I
try to reproduce the food,

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.510
I don't have all of the
ingredients available

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.210
and suddenly I have to start
making some substitutions

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.490
and all of a sudden
my food can end up

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:56.020
looking a little more
something like this.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.810
And I find that the same can be
said for a lot of programming

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.280
tutorials.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.650
If you have all of the right
ingredients measured out

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.720
in the exact right
proportions, then it's

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.300
pretty easy to follow
along with a simple recipe

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.250
and get the desired results.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.500
But if you're missing
some key ingredients,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.510
then you start having to
make some substitutions

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.840
or find some workarounds.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.382
And then you'll end
up with something

00:02:16.382 --> 00:02:19.900
that's perhaps technically
edible, although certainly less

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.700
than ideal.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.875
And that leads me to the
outline for my talk today.

00:02:24.875 --> 00:02:27.250
I'm going to be discussing
how to incorporate these three

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.170
languages into your
workflow, but I'm

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.530
going to present it
in two different ways.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.028
The first way is
an ideal approach,

00:02:35.028 --> 00:02:37.570
and this approach is going to
be kind of like following along

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:41.680
with a simple three step recipe
where but the downside here

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.530
is that I'm going to assume that
you have all of the ingredients

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.790
available.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.500
And the second way
that I'll present

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.630
is a less than
ideal approach where

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.220
you're missing some
key ingredients,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.770
and so you have to
find some workarounds.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:55.600
But before I jump
into that, I first

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.850
want to start by providing
some context as to why

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.375
this particular
challenge arose for me.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.760
I'm an analyst at
Statistics Canada, which,

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.700
for those who are unfamiliar, is
an agency within the government

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:09.580
of Canada.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.370
And Statistics
Canada is Canada's

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.630
official statistical agency.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:16.360
And the particular challenge
that I face at work

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.020
is that my employer wants to
adopt open source programming

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.670
languages like R and Python.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.280
But we have a legal
responsibility

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.770
to ensure that personal
information collected

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:32.870
for statistical purposes is
kept strictly confidential.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.000
This means that when
we publish statistics,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.270
we first need to use an internal
generalized system called

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.240
G-Confid, which is
a series of SAS macros

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.880
that prevents the release
of confidential information

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.250
in tabular data using
cell suppression

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.370
and controlled random
routing methods.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.910
And G-Confid is only
implemented in the SAS

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:54.680
programming language.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.880
And what this means in practice
is that many analysts and data

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.300
scientists at Statistics
Canada have to use SAS

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.750
as part of their workflow.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.860
If they want to be able to
publish official statistics.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.410
My typical workflow
at Statcan usually

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.540
looks a little bit
something like this.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:14.190
I work with some
SAS data sets

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.279
that first get passed through,
some pre-written preprocessing

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:19.589
scripts that are
written in SAS.

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:21.839
And once the data are
ready to be analyzed,

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.730
I bring the data
into R and then I

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:28.230
do the vast majority
of my analysis in R.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.540
But if I want those
results to be publishable,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.390
then I need to bring
the data from R back

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:37.410
into SAS so that I can
use these G-Config macros.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.590
And you can think of G-Config
kind of like a black box.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.160
It's a series of SAS macros that
is completely uneditable by me,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.920
and these macros are
themselves confidential.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.880
So I cannot and I would not
want to implement G-Config

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.770
in any other
programming language.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.670
So for this step, I
really do need to use SAS.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.460
Once I'm finished with
G-Config, I bring those results

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.700
back into R for data
visualization purposes.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:05.250
And as you can see,
my workflow really

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.400
does require me to be able to
go back and forth between SAS

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.470
and R seamlessly.

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.040
And while I myself don't
typically use Python at work,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.510
I do have a lot
of colleagues who

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.880
would appreciate the
ability to incorporate it

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.350
into their workflow.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.290
So all of this led
me to the question

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.480
is there a way to
combine these three

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.630
languages in the same
script in a way that

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.300
does not cause a headache?

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:31.750
And the answer is Yes.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.810
And there are multiple
different approaches.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.460
The most
straightforward approach

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:39.420
is a simple three-step recipe,
in step one of this recipe

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.270
is to gather your
required ingredients.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.530
For this particular recipe,

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.780
the required ingredients are
the languages that you're

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.310
interested in using, plus Java
and the other ingredient that's

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.990
not pictured here,
but which really

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.940
is the most important
ingredient for making

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.750
this particular recipe
succeed, is admin privileges.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.630
And if you don't have admin
privileges on your computer,

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:04.760
you might not be able to
get this recipe to work.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.890
Step two of this
three-step recipe,

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:09.680
assuming you have all of those
required ingredients from step

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.650
one, is to install
SASPy, which is

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:16.160
a Python package that works
very similarly to how reticulate

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.210
works.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:21.590
So just like reticulate is
an R interface to Python, SASPy

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:26.550
is a Python
interface to SAS.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.880
The third and final step
of this three-step recipe

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.873
is to perform a couple
of system configurations,

00:06:32.873 --> 00:06:35.290
and there's a lot of little
details that are in this step.

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:37.630
So I won't go over that
today for the sake of time.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:41.100
But if you're interested
in following along,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.140
there is a video on
my YouTube channel

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.930
that explains all of the
steps and includes an example

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.140
usage of combining these three
languages in the same script

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:52.250
using SASPy.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.870
Now that was definitely the
most straightforward approach.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.500
And the recommended well,
my personal recommended way

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.270
of how to achieve this workflow.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.850
But if you have limited
control over your computer,

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:03.560
then it might not
be possible for you.

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.060
So in the next few
slides, I'm going

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.640
to present a few
alternative approaches that

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:09.830
will allow you to
combine SAS and R

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:13.630
in the same script
and sometimes Python.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.150
The approach that you choose
is going to depend heavily

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.190
on what you need to do
and on how much control

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:20.020
you have over your computer.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.210
And for the three
alternative options

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.360
that I'm about to present,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.943
for all three
of them, there's

00:07:24.943 --> 00:07:30.480
going to be a big trade-off between
complexity and usefulness.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.227
The first option is to use the
SASmarkdown package, which,

00:07:34.227 --> 00:07:36.060
as you might be able
to guess from the name,

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.130
is a package that
allows you to include

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:42.510
SAS chunks in your R Markdown
or Quarto documents.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.710
This option has the
benefit of not requiring

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.020
admin privileges,
but the downside

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:51.340
is that there's no interaction
between SAS and R at all.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.860
So what this means is
that the SAS data sets

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.050
that you define
in your SAS chunks

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:57.700
are not going to be
accessible to you

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.490
in your R chunks and vice versa.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:03.460
For me personally, I really
need this interoperability

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.880
between these two languages.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.230
So this particular option
isn't the most useful for me,

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:11.800
but this package does have some
really interesting use cases.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.170
For example, if you're
a course instructor,

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.720
you could use this in order
to show your students how

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.780
to perform the same task using
multiple different programming

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.720
languages all in the same place.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.270
Once you have the
SASmarkdown package installed,

00:08:25.270 --> 00:08:27.850
it's fairly easy to switch
between the three languages

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.980
within your R Markdown
or Quarto documents,

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:32.110
and all you need
to do is specify

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:36.690
which language you'd like to use
in each individual code chunk.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.720
If you want to use SAS,
just set the engine option

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:44.310
to be sashtml and you can
set the engine.path option

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:48.820
to be the file path to
your SAS executable.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:52.510
To use R, leave the chunk
header as the default.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.140
And to use Python, set the
chunk header to say Python.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.510
And if you were to knit an
R Markdown document that

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.730
contains three chunks
that looks like this,

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:04.010
the result will be
something that looks like.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:09.350
This the second approach
that you can use

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:13.880
is a SAS-first approach rather
than an R-irst approach.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.240
When SAS is launched, there's
an option that you can enable,

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.690
which is called RLANG and
the RLANG that you see there

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.090
on the slide in all
caps, I should mention,

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:27.180
has absolutely no relationship
to the rlang package in R.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:30.860
So this all caps rlang that
you see here is a SAS concept,

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:33.080
not an R concept.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:35.150
The RLANG option
can be enabled

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.880
without admin privileges
and it's pretty easy

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.340
to move data back and forth
between SAS and R using

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.470
this method, which
makes this option quite

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.360
a bit more useful
than the SASmarkdown

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:48.470
package for my
purposes at least.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.510
Once the RALND
option is enabled,

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:54.740
you can use proc iml in
SAS to execute R code

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:56.690
and in order to
execute that R code,

00:09:56.690 --> 00:10:01.160
just use a submit statement with
the R option enabled and then

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.990
anything that you put
between the submit

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:05.990
and endsubmit lines will
be executed as R code

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.617
rather than as SAS code.

00:10:08.617 --> 00:10:10.700
Now I mentioned that it's
pretty easy to move data

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.770
back and forth
between R and SAS,

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.290
and if you want to
go from R to SAS,

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:18.860
you can use the ImportDataSetFromR
subroutine.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:21.140
And if you want to go in
the opposite direction,

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:24.890
so to go from SAS to
R, use the

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.020
ExportDataSetToR subroutine.

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:29.850
Both of these subroutines
use the exact same syntax.

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.510
You start by specifying the
name of the SAS data set

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:36.200
and then specify the
name of the R dataframe.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.360
I mentioned before that
this option doesn't

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.060
require admin privileges,
but really that's

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:42.740
only partially true.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:45.170
If you want to use this
method from base SAS,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.780
then you don't need to have
admin privileges in order

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.000
to enable this RLANG option.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.560
But if you're like
me, and if you

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.740
prefer to use an IDE like
SAS Enterprise Guide,

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.960
then you will need admin
privileges in order

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:59.660
to enable this feature.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:02.570
If you are interested in using
this method from within SAS

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:05.183
Enterprise Guide, there is a
video on my YouTube channel

00:11:05.183 --> 00:11:07.100
that goes through the
full instructions of how

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:09.750
to set this up.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.360
The third option that I'm
going to talk about today

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.600
is the one that is the
most complex, but also

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:16.960
the one that's the most useful.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.160
If what you really need is the
interoperability between SAS

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.830
and R, and if you don't
have admin privileges.

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:28.117
And if you want to be working
from within RStudio, and this

00:11:28.117 --> 00:11:30.450
is actually the approach that
I was using myself at work

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:32.070
for the past couple of months.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.093
Myself and a
coworker on my team,

00:11:34.093 --> 00:11:35.760
as well as a colleague
from another part

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.770
of our organization,
worked together

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:41.340
to develop an internal R package
that contains a function that

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:45.600
allows you to take an R data
frame, manipulate it in SAS

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.380
and then return the results
to R as in R data frame

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:53.080
without ever leaving RStudio.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:55.060
And this package is
not publicly available,

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:57.490
but you can easily come up
with a similar solution using

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.320
the same general
underlying principles

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:02.180
that I'm about to show you.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.100
The first argument that
this function takes

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:05.960
is the name of the
dataframe that you'd

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.060
like to manipulate in SAS.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.700
And here I'm just using
mtcars data set,

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.340
which is a data set that
comes built into R

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:15.290
and includes variables
about characteristics

00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:18.930
of cars such as miles per
gallon, horsepower cylinders,

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.917
and so on.

00:12:20.917 --> 00:12:22.750
The second argument
that this function takes

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:27.820
is the location to the SAS
installation on your machine.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:29.823
And the final argument
that this function takes

00:12:29.823 --> 00:12:31.240
is the SAS code
that you'd like

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.000
to apply to your dataframe.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.280
This function assumes
that in SAS, you

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:41.090
have access to a data
set that's called in_df,

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:43.790
and then the SAS data
set that will eventually

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:46.650
get sent back to the user as
an R data frame is called .

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.760
out_df.

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:51.700
And the example
that I'm using here,

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.220
I'm just using the
frequency procedure in SAS

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:57.640
to create a frequency table
of the cylinder variable

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.020
from the mtcars data set.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.810
And by executing just
those two lines of code,

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:07.250
the results from SAS are
brought to the user in RStudio

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:11.100
without ever having
had to exit RStudio.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:12.660
In terms of the
actual implementation

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:13.660
of this function,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.440
this function starts by
creating a temporary file folder

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:21.240
and then saving the user
inputted R dataframe as a dot

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.220
file in that
temporary file folder.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:27.280
From here, a temporary
SAS program is created

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:29.470
and the user inputted
SAS code

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.980
is then inserted into that
temporary SAS program, making

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:37.450
use of proc iml and the
ImportDataSetFrom R and 

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:39.250
ExportDataSetToR
subroutines that we talked

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.320
about a little bit earlier.

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:43.060
This temporary SAS
program is then

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:47.250
executed by sending a command
to the command prompt.

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.180
Enabling the RLANG option.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.080
And then the temporary files are
deleted after the results are

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:55.200
brought back into R
and finally the results

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.970
are returned to the
user as an R dataframe.

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:02.300
We all love

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:04.300
R but there might be
times where we want

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:08.150
or need to use other
programming languages.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:11.030
Tools like SASPy are
making this easier than ever

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.670
to combine multiple
languages in the same place.

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.730
But if these options are
unavailable or infeasible

00:14:16.730 --> 00:14:18.698
to you for whatever
reason, then you

00:14:18.698 --> 00:14:20.990
might be able to find some
clever workarounds if you're

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.830
willing to put in the work.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:26.230
For me, it's been a
little over a year

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.090
of exploring all these
different options

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:30.970
and more in order to figure
out what's viable for me

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:32.120
in my workplace.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:34.960
And I'm very happy to say
that as two weeks ago,

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:36.940
some changes to our
IT infrastructure

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:39.460
have resulted in Statistics
Canada employees being

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:44.220
able to use SASPy
without any issues at all.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:45.640
Thank you so much for listening.

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:48.600
I'd be super happy to connect
on LinkedIn or wherever

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.020
if you want to chat
about this more.

